<div class="needs"><span><strong>Download:</strong> <a href="../../code/talk-reveal.js.zip">talk-reveal.js.zip</a>, the presentation and its models (<code>demos/</code>), ready to show</span><span><strong>Show it:</strong> <code>python3 -m http.server</code> in its folder, then <code>http://localhost:8000/</code></span></div>

In reveal.js, a single HTML file contains the whole presentation, with one `<section>` per slide. The models
are placed in the directory `demos/` next to it:

```
reveal.js/
  index.html             the presentation, below
  demos/
    a1/ a2/ c3/ c6/ c7/  the web builds of the examples: index.html, project.js, project.wasm, project.data
    slide-controls.js    the buttons of the slides
```

- **A slide with a model** is a `<div class="side">`, with the text on the left and the frame on the right.
- **data-src** loads a model when its slide comes near, and unloads it after.
  - This way, the models of the other slides do not run in the background.
- All the slides are in the same page: the buttons of a slide therefore name their model (`data-demo="#surface"`).
- *Note: at the end of the file, a few lines give the keyboard back to the presentation when the slide changes
  (a model that has just been unloaded would otherwise keep it).*

