<div class="needs"><span><strong>Download:</strong> <a href="../../code/talk-lhtml-site.zip">talk-lhtml-site.zip</a>, the sources of the presentation and its models (<code>src/_demo/</code>)</span><span><strong>Build it:</strong> <code>lhtml-site</code> in its folder, then <code>lhtml-site --serve</code></span></div>

In lhtml-site, each slide is a folder, and the slides are ordered by the names of the folders. The models are
placed in `src/_demo/`.

*Rem. A folder whose name starts with an underscore is not a slide: it is copied as it is.*

```
lhtml-site/
  configure.yaml               the build: the slides in the browser, and a PDF
  src/
    01_title/index.html.j2     a slide: its title (=), its text, its model (demo::)
    02_outline/index.html.j2
    ...
    12_conclusion/index.html.j2
    _style/talk.css            the buttons of the slides, and the image of a model in the PDF
    _demo/a1/ ... _demo/c7/    the web builds of the examples, with their poster
    _demo/slide-controls.js
```

- **A slide with a model** uses the layout `side`, which places the text on the left and the block `media::`
  on the right.
- **demo::** writes the frame, and **img::** gives the image of the model used in the PDF.
  - The classes `.live` and `.still`, defined in `_style/talk.css`, show the frame on screen and the image in print.
- Each slide is a separate page: the buttons of a slide drive its model without having to name it.
- `configure.yaml` loads the stylesheet and `slide-controls.js` in every slide (keywords `cssAbs` and `jsAbs`).

